# serializer version: 1
# name: test_exit_codes
  dict({
    'EXIT_ERROR (implicit)': 1,
    'EXIT_EXTERNAL_WRITE': 4,
    'EXIT_NOT_INITIALIZED': 2,
    'EXIT_SUCCESS (implicit)': 0,
    'EXIT_VERSION_AHEAD': 3,
  })
# ---
# name: test_help[saltation apply]
  '''
  
   Usage: saltation apply [OPTIONS]
  
   Apply ``current.sql`` against the configured catalog.
  
   Idempotent mode is the default. Reset mode (``--reset`` or
   ``.mode = "reset"``) is the opt-in escape hatch for evolutions
   that genuinely cannot be expressed idempotently: it rolls the affected
   tables back to their last checkpoint, then re-applies. Idempotent is
   the path; reset is the relief valve.
  
  ╭─ Options ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
  │ --path           -p      PATH  Path to the saltation config TOML. [default: saltation.toml]                          │
  │ --allow-partial                Continue past statement failures and emit a partial-success event. Without this flag, │
  │                                the first failure surfaces immediately.                                               │
  │ --reset                        Run in reset mode: roll the affected tables back to their last checkpoint, then       │
  │                                re-apply. Use ONLY for evolutions that genuinely cannot be expressed idempotently —   │
  │                                idempotent is the default and the safety contract.                                    │
  │ --force                        Override external-write detection when using --reset. Required only if another writer │
  │                                has advanced one of the affected tables since the last saltation apply; using this    │
  │                                risks clobbering their work.                                                          │
  │ --json                         Emit machine-readable JSON instead of the human view.                                 │
  │ --help                         Show this message and exit.                                                           │
  ╰──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯
  
  '''
# ---
# name: test_help[saltation commit]
  '''
  
   Usage: saltation commit [OPTIONS]
  
   Freeze ``current.sql`` as a committed evolution.
  
   Preconditions: ``current.sql`` is non-empty and applies cleanly (run
   ``saltation apply`` first). The current text is moved to
   ``committed/<NNNN>_<slug>.sql``, the affected tables get a checkpoint
   pinned at the ``saltation.committed.<n>.<slug>`` tag, and ``current.sql``
   is truncated for the next dev iteration.
  
  ╭─ Options ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
  │ *  --message  -m      TEXT  Short description of the committed evolution (becomes the file slug). [required]         │
  │    --path     -p      PATH  Path to the saltation config TOML. [default: saltation.toml]                             │
  │    --json                   Emit machine-readable JSON output.                                                       │
  │    --help                   Show this message and exit.                                                              │
  ╰──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯
  
  '''
# ---
# name: test_help[saltation config check]
  '''
  
   Usage: saltation config check [OPTIONS]
  
   Validate the config file. Exits 0 on success, non-zero with a message on failure.
  
  ╭─ Options ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
  │ --path  -p      PATH  Path to the saltation config TOML. [default: saltation.toml]                                   │
  │ --help                Show this message and exit.                                                                    │
  ╰──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯
  
  '''
# ---
# name: test_help[saltation config show]
  '''
  
   Usage: saltation config show [OPTIONS]
  
   Print the (redacted) effective config.
  
   Credential-shaped values (token, secret, password, credential, *_key
   patterns) are replaced with ``"***"``. See ``saltation.config.redacted_dump``
   for the heuristic.
  
  ╭─ Options ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
  │ --path  -p      PATH  Path to the saltation config TOML. [default: saltation.toml]                                   │
  │ --json                Emit machine-readable JSON instead of the human view.                                          │
  │ --help                Show this message and exit.                                                                    │
  ╰──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯
  
  '''
# ---
# name: test_help[saltation config]
  '''
  
   Usage: saltation config [OPTIONS] COMMAND [ARGS]...
  
   Inspect and validate the saltation config file.
  
  ╭─ Options ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
  │ --help          Show this message and exit.                                                                          │
  ╰──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯
  ╭─ Commands ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
  │ show   Print the (redacted) effective config.                                                                        │
  │ check  Validate the config file. Exits 0 on success, non-zero with a message on failure.                             │
  ╰──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯
  
  '''
# ---
# name: test_help[saltation download]
  '''
  
   Usage: saltation download [OPTIONS]
  
   Alias for ``saltation sync``. Same semantics; chosen for muscle memory.
  
   Postgres-land tools tend to spell this ``download`` (sqitch) or ``sync``
   (graphile-migrate); we accept both. The catalog is the source of truth
   and both commands rehydrate the local ``committed/`` directory from it.
  
  ╭─ Options ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
  │ --path     -p      PATH  Path to the saltation config TOML. [default: saltation.toml]                                │
  │ --dry-run                Plan only; write nothing to disk.                                                           │
  │ --diff                   Print a unified diff of local vs catalog on conflicts.                                      │
  │ --force                  Overwrite local committed files on conflict.                                                │
  │ --json                   Emit a single-line JSON summary.                                                            │
  │ --help                   Show this message and exit.                                                                 │
  ╰──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯
  
  '''
# ---
# name: test_help[saltation init]
  '''
  
   Usage: saltation init [OPTIONS]
  
   Bootstrap the ``_saltation`` sidecar in the configured catalog.
  
   Idempotent: re-running on an already-initialised catalog is a no-op.
   Fails if the sidecar is at a *newer* version than this binary knows.
  
  ╭─ Options ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
  │ --path  -p      PATH  Path to the saltation config TOML. [default: saltation.toml]                                   │
  │ --help                Show this message and exit.                                                                    │
  ╰──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯
  
  '''
# ---
# name: test_help[saltation redo]
  '''
  
   Usage: saltation redo [OPTIONS]
  
   Alias for ``saltation apply --reset``.
  
   Same semantics, shorter to type during a non-idempotent-evolution
   dev loop. Reset is the relief valve — prefer idempotent SQL.
  
  ╭─ Options ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
  │ --path           -p      PATH  Path to the saltation config TOML. [default: saltation.toml]                          │
  │ --allow-partial                Continue past statement failures.                                                     │
  │ --force                        Override external-write detection. See `saltation apply --reset --help`.              │
  │ --json                         Emit machine-readable JSON output.                                                    │
  │ --help                         Show this message and exit.                                                           │
  ╰──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯
  
  '''
# ---
# name: test_help[saltation reset-to]
  '''
  
   Usage: saltation reset-to [OPTIONS] EVOLUTION_ID
  
   Roll affected tables back to a committed evolution's checkpoint. INCIDENT RESPONSE ONLY — does not delete committed
   files, does not touch downstream commit events. Reset is the relief valve, never the recommended fix; reach for
   idempotent SQL first.
  
  ╭─ Arguments ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
  │ *    evolution_id      TEXT  The committed evolution id to roll back to, e.g. '0007_add_users'. See `saltation       │
  │                              status` for the live set.                                                               │
  │                              [required]                                                                              │
  ╰──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯
  ╭─ Options ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
  │ --path  -p      PATH  Path to the saltation config TOML. [default: saltation.toml]                                   │
  │ --json                Emit machine-readable JSON output.                                                             │
  │ --help                Show this message and exit.                                                                    │
  ╰──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯
  
  '''
# ---
# name: test_help[saltation rollback]
  '''
  
   Usage: saltation rollback [OPTIONS]
  
   Roll the affected tables of the last apply back to their checkpoint, without re-applying.
  
   Useful when you want to discard a dev iteration's table mutations
   entirely and edit ``current.sql`` from scratch. Does not re-execute
   SQL afterwards — the next ``saltation apply`` re-applies the (possibly
   edited) ``current.sql``.
  
  ╭─ Options ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
  │ --path  -p      PATH  Path to the saltation config TOML. [default: saltation.toml]                                   │
  │ --json                Emit machine-readable JSON output.                                                             │
  │ --help                Show this message and exit.                                                                    │
  ╰──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯
  
  '''
# ---
# name: test_help[saltation status]
  '''
  
   Usage: saltation status [OPTIONS]
  
   Report sidecar version, committed evolutions, and the current in-flight apply.
  
   Read-only: tolerates a sidecar that is one or more versions ahead of
   this binary (prints a warning). Exits ``2`` if the sidecar has never
   been initialised in this catalog.
  
  ╭─ Options ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
  │ --path  -p      PATH  Path to the saltation config TOML. [default: saltation.toml]                                   │
  │ --json                Emit machine-readable JSON instead of the human view.                                          │
  │ --help                Show this message and exit.                                                                    │
  ╰──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯
  
  '''
# ---
# name: test_help[saltation sync]
  '''
  
   Usage: saltation sync [OPTIONS]
  
   Rehydrate the local ``committed/`` directory from the catalog.
  
   Read-only against the catalog: no events are written. Missing files are
   created, hash-matching files are skipped, and files whose content differs
   from the catalog row are refused unless ``--force`` is passed. Use
   ``--diff`` to inspect conflicts; use ``--dry-run`` to plan without
   touching the filesystem. Exits ``4`` if any conflict was refused so CI
   can fail loud on a drifted checkout.
  
  ╭─ Options ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
  │ --path     -p      PATH  Path to the saltation config TOML. [default: saltation.toml]                                │
  │ --dry-run                Plan only; write nothing to disk.                                                           │
  │ --diff                   Print a unified diff of local vs catalog on conflicts. Implies --dry-run unless --force is  │
  │                          also passed.                                                                                │
  │ --force                  Overwrite local committed files that conflict with the catalog.                             │
  │ --json                   Emit a single-line JSON summary.                                                            │
  │ --help                   Show this message and exit.                                                                 │
  ╰──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯
  
  '''
# ---
# name: test_help[saltation uncommit]
  '''
  
   Usage: saltation uncommit [OPTIONS]
  
   Pop the latest committed evolution back into ``current.sql``.
  
   Rolls the affected tables back to the checkpoint pinned at commit time
   and deletes the committed file. Refuses if downstream committed files
   exist (a gap in numbering would be a corruption) or if ``current.sql``
   is non-empty without ``--force``.
  
  ╭─ Options ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
  │ --path   -p      PATH  Path to the saltation config TOML. [default: saltation.toml]                                  │
  │ --force                Overwrite a non-empty current.sql with the uncommitted content. Without --force, uncommit     │
  │                        refuses to clobber unsaved work.                                                              │
  │ --json                 Emit machine-readable JSON output.                                                            │
  │ --help                 Show this message and exit.                                                                   │
  ╰──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯
  
  '''
# ---
# name: test_help[saltation watch]
  '''
  
   Usage: saltation watch [OPTIONS]
  
   Watch the evolutions directory and re-apply ``current.sql`` on every change.
  
   Default mode is idempotent — the safety contract. ``--reset`` (or
   ``.mode = "reset"``) enables the rollback-before-reapply path
   for non-idempotent evolutions. Use it sparingly. A parse or dispatch
   error during a re-apply is reported and the loop keeps watching.
  
  ╭─ Options ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
  │ --path           -p      PATH     Path to the saltation config TOML. [default: saltation.toml]                       │
  │ --debounce-ms            INTEGER  Override ``.debounce_ms`` for this invocation. Milliseconds of quiet time required │
  │                                   before a batch of edits triggers an apply.                                         │
  │ --allow-partial                   Continue past statement failures and emit a partial-success event.                 │
  │ --reset                           Run each re-apply in reset mode. Use ONLY for evolutions that genuinely cannot be  │
  │                                   expressed idempotently. Honours ``.mode = "reset"`` from saltation.toml as well.   │
  │ --force                           Override external-write detection in reset mode. See `saltation apply --help`.     │
  │ --json                            Emit one JSON line per watch event.                                                │
  │ --help                            Show this message and exit.                                                        │
  ╰──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯
  
  '''
# ---
# name: test_help[saltation]
  '''
  
   Usage: saltation [OPTIONS] COMMAND [ARGS]...
  
   SQL-driven evolution runner for Apache Iceberg tables.
  
  ╭─ Options ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
  │ --version          Show the saltation version and exit.                                                              │
  │ --help             Show this message and exit.                                                                       │
  ╰──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯
  ╭─ Commands ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
  │ init      Bootstrap the ``_saltation`` sidecar in the configured catalog.                                            │
  │ status    Report sidecar version, committed evolutions, and the current in-flight apply.                             │
  │ apply     Apply ``current.sql`` against the configured catalog.                                                      │
  │ redo      Alias for ``saltation apply --reset``.                                                                     │
  │ rollback  Roll the affected tables of the last apply back to their checkpoint, without re-applying.                  │
  │ commit    Freeze ``current.sql`` as a committed evolution.                                                           │
  │ uncommit  Pop the latest committed evolution back into ``current.sql``.                                              │
  │ reset-to  Roll affected tables back to a committed evolution's checkpoint. INCIDENT RESPONSE ONLY — does not delete  │
  │           committed files, does not touch downstream commit events. Reset is the relief valve, never the recommended │
  │           fix; reach for idempotent SQL first.                                                                       │
  │ watch     Watch the evolutions directory and re-apply ``current.sql`` on every change.                               │
  │ sync      Rehydrate the local ``committed/`` directory from the catalog.                                             │
  │ download  Alias for ``saltation sync``. Same semantics; chosen for muscle memory.                                    │
  │ config    Inspect and validate the saltation config file.                                                            │
  ╰──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯
  
  '''
